  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13941629/assets/737052513985954a32dcce369fce60f898474db846319ca31b36e3e4682b4b37.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-4$ is a root of odd multiplicity.\n- $p(0)=3.$\n- $x=1$ is a root of odd multiplicity.",
  "worked_solution": "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The root $x=-4$ has an even multiplicity because at $x=-4$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph passes through the $y$-axis at $y=3$, which means that $p(0)=3.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The root $x=1$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at the point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
